    <div class="faq-section" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">

      <!-- ── SECTION 1: RECOGNISING AN EMERGENCY ── -->
      <h2 class="faq-category" style="margin-top:0">Recognising an Orthopedic Emergency</h2>

      <!-- Q1 -->
      <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
        <details open>
          <summary itemprop="name">What counts as an orthopedic emergency in Ahmedabad?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p itemprop="text">The following need immediate evaluation at an emergency department — not a wait-and-see approach:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Fractures</strong> — visible deformity, inability to bear weight, or a limb that looks "wrong"</li>
              <li><strong>Joint dislocations</strong> — shoulder, hip, knee, elbow, or finger</li>
              <li><strong>Open injuries</strong> — wounds where bone is visible or exposed</li>
              <li><strong>Road-accident polytrauma</strong> — multiple injuries from a two-wheeler or car accident</li>
              <li><strong>Crush injuries</strong> — from machinery, falling objects, or vehicle accidents</li>
              <li><strong>Sudden inability to move or bear weight</strong> on a limb after a fall, even without an obvious deformity</li>
            </ul>
            <p>If in doubt, call and describe the injury — our team will advise whether to come in immediately or by ambulance.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <!-- Q2 -->
      <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
        <details>
          <summary itemprop="name">Is emergency orthopedic and trauma care available 24/7 at DHS Hospital?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p itemprop="text">Yes. DHS Multispecialty Hospital's trauma service runs round the clock, 365 days a year, backed by a 24/7 ICU and standby operation theatres so a case can move straight from the emergency desk into surgery when needed. Call the emergency helpline at <strong>+91 9081610444</strong> any time, day or night — landline back-up is available on (079) 26844444 / (079) 26845555.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <!-- ── SECTION 2: AMBULANCE & ARRIVAL ── -->
      <h2 class="faq-category">Ambulance &amp; Getting to Hospital</h2>

      <!-- Q3 -->
      <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
        <details>
          <summary itemprop="name">Does DHS Hospital have its own ambulance service?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p itemprop="text">Yes. DHS operates a 24/7 ambulance service equipped with advanced life-support systems and staffed by trained paramedical personnel, so injured patients are stabilised and monitored on the way in, not just transported.</p>
            <p>To request an ambulance, call the emergency helpline at <strong>+91 9081610444</strong> and share the location and nature of the injury — dispatch begins immediately.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <!-- Q4 -->
      <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
        <details>
          <summary itemprop="name">What should I do immediately after a fall or accident, before reaching the hospital?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p itemprop="text">While help is on the way:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Keep the person still.</strong> Do not try to straighten or realign a visibly deformed limb.</li>
              <li><strong>Support, don't force.</strong> If the limb must be moved, support the joint above and below the injury.</li>
              <li><strong>Don't give food or water</strong> — emergency surgery may need to follow, and an empty stomach is safer for anaesthesia.</li>
              <li><strong>Call an ambulance instead of self-transporting</strong> for a suspected hip, spine, or head injury — incorrect handling can worsen the damage.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring ID, insurance details, and current medications</strong> if you can, to speed up registration and billing.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Call <strong>+91 9081610444</strong> on the way — our team can advise you en route and have the trauma team ready when you arrive.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <!-- ── SECTION 3: TRAUMA TEAM & TREATMENT ── -->
      <h2 class="faq-category">Trauma Team &amp; Fracture Treatment Protocols</h2>

      <!-- Q5 -->
      <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
        <details>
          <summary itemprop="name">Who is on the trauma team when I arrive at DHS Hospital?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p itemprop="text">Orthopedic trauma care is led by <a href="/doctors/doctor-hardik/"><strong>Dr. Hardik M Shah</strong></a> (Medical Director, MBBS, MS Orthopedic, FRCS Germany) and <strong>Dr. Swagat M Shah</strong>, our orthopedic and trauma surgeons. For complex or multi-injury cases, they are supported round the clock by on-call neurosurgery, vascular surgery, and critical-care/anaesthesia specialists, along with 24/7 radiology (X-ray/CT) and ICU teams — so a single accident with several injuries is managed by one coordinated team rather than being referred out.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <!-- Q6 -->
      <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
        <details>
          <summary itemprop="name">How quickly will a fracture actually be treated?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p itemprop="text">Timelines depend on the type of fracture:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>On arrival:</strong> immediate triage and X-ray/imaging</li>
              <li><strong>Closed fractures</strong> (skin intact): assessed and a treatment plan finalised within hours</li>
              <li><strong>Open fractures</strong> (bone breaking the skin): taken up for surgical washout within 6 hours of arrival to reduce infection risk</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Calling ahead on <strong>+91 9081610444</strong> lets the trauma team prepare the OT and blood bank in advance, which shortens the time from arrival to treatment.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <!-- Q7 -->
      <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
        <details>
          <summary itemprop="name">Does DHS Hospital handle serious road accident and polytrauma cases?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p itemprop="text">Yes. DHS provides round-the-clock polytrauma care for patients with multiple injuries — for example, a fracture combined with a head or internal injury from a road accident. Orthopedic, neurosurgery, vascular, and critical-care specialists coordinate on the same case, supported by a 24/7 ICU and operation theatres kept on standby for exactly this kind of arrival.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <!-- ── SECTION 4: COST & INSURANCE ── -->
      <h2 class="faq-category">Cost &amp; Insurance for Emergency Care</h2>

      <!-- Q8 -->
      <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
        <details>
          <summary itemprop="name">Is cashless treatment available for orthopedic emergencies?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <p itemprop="text">DHS Multispecialty Hospital is empaneled with various health insurance providers, and our billing team assists with cashless approvals for emergency admissions. Call the billing desk at <strong>+91 9081021444</strong>, or simply tell the emergency team on arrival that you'd like to start a cashless claim — they will guide the paperwork alongside treatment. See our <a href="/cashless/">cashless insurance process</a> for details.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

